{"summary":"Revised `draft-03` around the supplied comments: the protagonist now stays because he likes the clearing, finds and names Moss during the opening montage, discovers the ravine while mining, and receives less explanatory narration. The granite seam remains as a functional visual coordinate, fire foreshadowing is quieter, and the climax uses a muffled remembered bark. All four permitted internal records are updated; performance and mechanics evidence remains pending.","document":"# Production script — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n> Draft ID: `draft-03`. This revision implements the supplied commented notes within the current unlocked echo-reality proposal. It records no creative lock, table read, mechanics test, animatic result or production validation.\n\n## Status and use\n\n- Editorial target: approximately 20 minutes. Scene timings are planning guides, not measured results or structural quotas.\n- `R0` to `R4` are crew labels and are never spoken.\n- One creator-voiced, third-person narrator stays close to the protagonist's understanding. The on-screen protagonist does not speak.\n- Crying-obsidian portal behaviour is invented story canon. Seamless portal views are planned with the Immersive Portals mod; compatibility and capture behaviour have not been verified for the selected Minecraft version.\n- Fire spread, wolf behaviour, creeper blocking, charged-anchor staging and surviving-frame geometry must be tested before capture.\n\n## Continuity keys\n\n- **MATCH PATH** uses the same camera position, lens, path and direction of travel in Scenes 1 and 2. R4 contains the developed homestead and smoke; R0 contains the fresh clearing.\n- **MATCH FRAME A** is the pre-mining composition near the end of Scene 2, after Moss has been found, tamed and named. Scene 13 repeats the exact image and narration recording.\n- The forked, lightning-shaped granite seam is a visual coordinate, not a lore symbol. Frame it consistently in every echo so viewers can recognise place before the Atlas names the pattern.\n- The protagonist's survey journal begins mostly blank, with a stitched corner and three-line ravine symbol. The Weeping Atlas is a weathered counterpart with the same repair, symbol and handwriting.\n- The protagonist arrives at the clearing alone. He finds and tames the wolf during Scene 2. The crew name is Moss throughout, but no in-world name appears above him until the name-tag beat.\n- Moss's rug is moss-green wool. It grows one square at a time as he edges closer to the hearth; the final square later supplies the proposed fire path without receiving ominous narration.\n- Echo mouths use crying obsidian, directional tear-like particles and a low layered sound heard only after discovery. Their centres show seamless destination views rather than opaque fields.\n- The conventional Nether portal uses ordinary obsidian and familiar audio.\n- The completed R4 frame is the source that projects the R0 receiving mouth. A builder-keyed route targets the first threshold; an unkeyed entrant at R0 continues forwards to R1.\n- Moss's R4 fate remains unseen. Show no body, collar drop or death message.\n- The falling creeper must have a readable dark route from the ledge glimpsed in Scene 11. Its blast waking the charged echo frame is invented canon, not a claimed vanilla mechanic.\n\n---\n\n### 1. EXT. R4 RIDGE / HOMESTEAD PATH — NIGHT\n\n*Editorial target: 0:18*\n\n**Visual intent.** Open on smoke, urgent movement and unexplained purple cargo. End on a path composition that can cut directly to the same place in R0.\n\n**Audio intent.** Wind, hurried movement and distant fire. No narration, music or portal audio before the cut.\n\n**Action**\n\nNight. The protagonist crests the ridge from the direction of a conventional Nether portal. Crying obsidian and glowstone fill his pack and hands.\n\nHe sees smoke before he sees the house.\n\nHe drops the cargo beside the path and runs.\n\nFrom **MATCH PATH**, the camera watches him run away towards the burning homestead.\n\nJump cut on his movement into Scene 2.\n\n**Production note.** Preserve the exact camera position, lens, path line and direction of travel. Scene 10 resumes the emergency from the end of this shot.\n\n---\n\n### 2. EXT. R0 CLEARING ABOVE THE RAVINE — MORNING / PASSAGE OF DAYS\n\n*Editorial target: 3:05*\n\n**Visual intent.** Replace the later emergency with an unhurried arrival. Let affection for the place precede utility, then build the protagonist's relationship with Moss through ordinary survival routines.\n\n**Audio intent.** Cut the distant fire to morning wind, leaves and footsteps. Let work sounds carry the montage. Do not use portal sound to draw him towards the ravine.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe jump cut keeps the exact **MATCH PATH** camera. Morning replaces night. The developed R4 landscape is now an untouched clearing.\n\nOn the same path, the protagonist walks into frame alone with simpler equipment and a mostly blank survey journal.\n\nTitle over the matched shot: **THE PORTAL THAT NOBODY MADE**\n\nAt the overlook, he lowers his pack. Water catches the morning light below. Birch leaves move over a wide patch of grass. He could continue along the ridge, but instead sits for a moment and watches the clearing.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He could have kept walking. The light was good, the water was close, and for once nothing asked him to hurry. He liked the place. So he put his pack down.\n\n**EARLY-GAME MONTAGE — SEVERAL MINECRAFT DAYS**\n\n- He cuts oak and birch, makes basic tools and raises a small shelter.\n- He draws the clearing and a simple three-line ravine symbol in the stitched journal.\n- At night, he clears a zombie from the doorway, a spider from the crop edge and a skeleton from beneath the birches.\n- On the following morning, an untamed wolf watches from the same tree line.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> By the third night, the clearing had a door. By the fourth, something waited outside it that was not trying to kill him.\n\nThe protagonist lowers his tool, approaches slowly and offers bones recovered from the skeleton. Hearts rise; the wolf's collar appears.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> A few bones settled the question. The wolf stayed.\n\nThe montage continues:\n\n- The wolf follows while the protagonist plants wheat and later leads two cows into a simple pen.\n- He waits on the bank while the protagonist fishes, then repeatedly settles on the damp green patch beneath the birches.\n- A name tag eventually comes up on the line.\n- After gathering enough iron for an anvil, the protagonist glances from the blank tag to the green bank and labels it **MOSS**.\n- He uses it on the wolf. The name appears above him for the first time.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The river supplied a blank tag. The bank supplied the name.\n\n- The shelter gains a bed, chest, furnace and roof.\n- Moss waits at the door during rain, occupies useful construction blocks and follows the protagonist on short gathering routes.\n- The protagonist records the starter build and their explored paths in his journal.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The shelter grew around two sets of habits.\n\nLater, the furnace consumes the last iron ore and the current pickaxe is nearly spent. The protagonist packs torches, food, a bucket and the journal for an ordinary mining descent.\n\nAt **MATCH FRAME A**, he tightens the pack above the ravine. Moss trots into position beside him. Neither pauses to listen; nothing calls them below.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O. — RECORDING TO REUSE IN SCENE 13)**\n\n> Below the hill was only another day's work. They went together.\n\nThe protagonist starts down. Moss follows once the first ledge is safe.\n\n**Production note.** Save **MATCH FRAME A**, including camera, lens, sun direction, equipment, Moss position and narration take, for Scene 13. Fishing a name tag is a planned capture beat, not a recorded probability test.\n\n---\n\n### 3. INT. R0 RAVINE CAVE — DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:35*\n\n**Visual intent.** Introduce the granite seam as an ordinary route marker before it becomes the shared coordinate across echoes. Make the portal a physical opening whose destination can already be inspected.\n\n**Audio intent.** Begin with mining, water and paws. Introduce the unfamiliar low portal texture only after the frame enters view; it does not cause the discovery.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist descends by water and short block placements. Moss waits at a narrow ledge until the route is safe.\n\nAt mining depth, a forked granite seam crosses the grey wall like a lightning stroke. The protagonist sketches its outline beside the shaft depth in his journal, then follows an exposed iron vein beyond it.\n\nAround the next turn stands a complete crying-obsidian frame. Tear-like particles travel consistently towards its far face.\n\nThe centre is a seamless view into what first resembles the same cave. Rain falls through an opening on the far side while the stone above the protagonist remains dry.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> It was crying obsidian: a frame he had no recipe for, standing open.\n\nThe camera moves laterally. The destination shifts with natural parallax. There is no reflected protagonist. Beyond the frame, the same forked granite seam carries a stream of rainwater.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Beyond it, rain fell through a ceiling that was dry above his head.\n\nThe protagonist circles the frame. He checks the adjoining tunnels, ceiling and floor. Each route dead-ends or returns to his new mine; there is no scaffold, camp or second access shaft.\n\nMoss approaches, then looks back at him.\n\nThe protagonist brings Moss beside the threshold and watches the rain for one final moment.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He had come down for iron. Curiosity took him one step farther.\n\nThey cross in one continuous movement. Dry cave audio gives way to rain at the threshold.\n\n**Production note.** The destination view, matched parallax, wolf transfer and continuous crossing depend on unverified mod and capture behaviour. A controlled cut or composite remains acceptable if the geography is preserved.\n\n---\n\n### 4. INT./EXT. R1 RAIN ECHO — DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:30*\n\n**Visual intent.** Confirm the visible destination, then let the missing surface home challenge the assumption that the doorway only crossed space.\n\n**Audio intent.** Continue the rain heard through the opening. Keep the portal texture beneath it without using a musical reveal.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist and Moss complete their step beside the same forked granite seam in the same camera composition.\n\nRainwater pours through the cave roof. A waterfall occupies the route that was dry. Dense vegetation covers the surface. The shelter, crops, cow pen and marked paths are absent.\n\nThe protagonist checks his coordinates and compares the seam sketch with the wall.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The doorway had told the truth about the rain. It had said nothing about the missing house.\n\nAt the surface, the ravine profile remains recognisable beneath different tree growth and water paths.\n\nBack at the frame, the opening shows a dry cave. The missing surface build is outside the limited view.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The opening offered dry stone. He read it as a return.\n\nBefore crossing, the protagonist places one cobblestone block against the left foot of the R1 frame and sketches its position.\n\nHe and Moss step into the dry view.\n\nOn the far side, grey ash drifts through still air.\n\n---\n\n### 5. INT./EXT. R2 ASH ECHO TO R3 ARCHIVE ECHO — LATE DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:30*\n\n**Visual intent.** Turn the failed return into a deliberate experiment. Identical close framing must let the missing marks establish the rule before narration names its consequence.\n\n**Audio intent.** Replace rain with dry wind and distant debris. Use one restrained musical pulse only after the second mark is absent.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist turns immediately towards the left frame foot. The cobblestone block is absent.\n\nAt the surface, a scorched tree line stands above an exposed ravine shelf. None of the R0 construction is present.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The rain was behind him. Home was not.\n\nHe checks the journal, then constructs a more deliberate mark: two cobblestone blocks in an L, with a torch on the outer block. He records the arrangement and his remaining block count.\n\nThrough the portal, a darker cave contains an old survey recess. The protagonist looks once more at the L-shaped mark before crossing specifically to test whether it follows.\n\nIn the Archive Echo, the matching frame foot is bare.\n\nHold the repeated close-up long enough for the absence to register.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Once could be loss. Twice was a rule.\n\nThe protagonist compares coordinates, seam sketches and the two marker drawings.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Same numbers. Wrong history.\n\nHe writes one word beneath the sketches: **ECHOES**.\n\n**Production note.** Do not rely on colour grading alone. Rain paths, vegetation, ash, constructed marks and the survey recess must distinguish the states.\n\n---\n\n### 6. INT. R3 ARCHIVE ECHO — DUSK\n\n*Editorial target: 1:55*\n\n**Visual intent.** Let the Atlas organise evidence already earned. Its final instruction remains useful, personal and incomplete rather than becoming a complete cosmology.\n\n**Audio intent.** Quiet the score for readable inserts. The destination view begins stable, then skips and darkens as the mouth fails.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe survey recess is old but arranged with familiar habits: torches on the same side, compact storage and the three-line ravine symbol cut into a desk.\n\nInside a protected drawer lies a weathered journal with the same stitched corner as the protagonist's book.\n\nHe lays the journals together. The repair, shorthand, ravine symbol and arrow-shaped number seven match.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The repair was his. So was the hand. The years between them were not.\n\n**ATLAS INSERTS — HOLD EACH LONG ENOUGH TO READ**\n\n1. Several plans of the identical ravine laid over one another.\n2. A chain of mouths with all unkeyed arrows pointing forwards: **NEXT MOUTH. NEVER PREVIOUS.**\n3. The phrase **ONE FRAME, MANY MOUTHS.**\n4. A complete crying-obsidian frame joined to a charged respawn anchor.\n5. The warning **BUILDER PRESENT AT IGNITION BECOMES THE KEY.**\n6. A keyed arrow towards **FIRST THRESHOLD** and a separate note: **UNKEYED ENTRY: NEXT MOUTH.**\n7. A stone baffle and blast-distance figure whose final measurement has been torn away.\n\nThe protagonist compares the chain drawing with his marker sketches and the particles entering the frame.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He trusted the pages that repeated what his own marks had proved.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Unkeyed, every arrow went forwards. Next mouth. Never previous.\n\nHe turns to the complete-frame diagram.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> One page offered a choice: complete the frame, charge an anchor, and be present when it woke. The builder would become the key to the first threshold.\n\nHis thumb reaches the torn edge of the safety diagram.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The safety figure ended at the tear.\n\nThe destination view skips. One layer of portal sound drops out. Gravel collapses into the old surface stair and shifts again when the protagonist attempts to clear it.\n\nMoss backs towards the shrinking opening. The protagonist takes both journals and chooses the remaining route.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The book offered a way back. The cave allowed only forwards.\n\nThey cross as the destination image contracts around them.\n\n**Production note.** These labels are the maximum planned portal exposition. Add nothing unless a later storyboard, animatic or comprehension review supplies specific evidence of confusion.\n\n---\n\n### 7. INT./EXT. R4 HOME ECHO — MORNING\n\n*Editorial target: 1:10*\n\n**Visual intent.** Make the new echo immediately survivable, then close the route and replace travel with the practical need for shelter.\n\n**Audio intent.** Begin with birds and stillness. Let the mouth collapse softly rather than explode.\n\n**Action**\n\nThey emerge into a dry, habitable ravine beside the same granite seam.\n\nBehind them, the destination view fractures. Projected upper blocks fade, leaving an incomplete arrangement of crying obsidian around empty air.\n\nThe protagonist tries ordinary fire. It burns briefly and goes out without opening a view.\n\nHe compares the dead mouth with the Atlas's full-frame diagram. His inventory lacks the required crying obsidian, charged anchor and cover.\n\nAt the surface are clean water, timber and open ground. He plants a marker directly above the dead mouth as evening approaches.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He could not build the way out before dark. He could build a roof.\n\nHe begins a small shelter. Moss settles inside just before nightfall.\n\n---\n\n### 8. EXT./INT. R4 HOMESTEAD — PASSAGE OF TIME\n\n*Editorial target: 2:30*\n\n**Visual intent.** Earn the house and relationship through shared routines. Place every material needed for the later fire in ordinary domestic context without announcing danger.\n\n**Audio intent.** Build a restrained theme from work sounds: block placement, furnace ticks, crops, weather and Moss's movement. Leave quiet holds around the domestic actions.\n\n**Action — MONTAGE IN REPEATED LOCKED COMPOSITIONS**\n\n- The first shelter gains a floor, walls and a compact upper storey.\n- Moss repeatedly stands on the exact block the protagonist intends to place. The protagonist lowers his tool and leaves that block until last.\n- Moss waits while a bridge is made safe, follows across and receives food after minor damage.\n- The protagonist builds a stone-backed open fireplace in the shared room.\n- Moss chooses the bare floor nearest its warmth. The protagonist lays a small moss-green wool rug beneath him.\n- During a storm, Moss goes directly to the rug and sleeps while thunder passes outside.\n- The protagonist keeps his journal and the weathered Atlas together. His own pages fill with echo diagrams, material counts and safer baffle designs.\n- A conventional obsidian Nether portal is built away from the ravine. Across several journeys, he returns with small amounts of crying obsidian and glowstone.\n- At the dead mouth, he measures space for a complete frame but lights nothing.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He called it a camp while leaving still sounded near.\n\nThe crops mature. Storage fills. Repeated shots show repairs, weather and use.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Temporary walls survived a season. Crops came in. Moss learnt which door opened first. The word camp quietly stopped fitting.\n\nMoss occupies another construction square. The protagonist waits, then builds around him.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Moss had a talent for choosing the one block still needed. The block could wait.\n\nAt the hearth, Moss lies with his front paws beyond the rug. On a later evening, the protagonist adds one green square beneath the place he has chosen. On another, Moss edges nearer the warmth and the rug grows by one final square.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The hearth chose Moss's corner. The rug followed him, one green square at a time.\n\nThe protagonist checks his material ledger. Only the last crying blocks and enough glowstone to finish charging the anchor remain.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He gathered a way out slowly enough to build a life around it.\n\n**Production note.** The final rug geometry supplies the proposed wool-to-timber route, but the edit should treat it as an affectionate household detail. Exact spread distance and timing require version-specific testing.\n\n---\n\n### 9. INT. R4 HOMESTEAD / CONVENTIONAL PORTAL — DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:05*\n\n**Visual intent.** Present leaving Moss as an established routine, not a warning delivered by dialogue. End on the consequence after the protagonist can no longer see it.\n\n**Audio intent.** Keep the domestic theme spare. After the conventional portal closes, leave only room tone, Moss and the hearth.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist marks the final two entries in his material ledger and packs for the Nether.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> One journey remained: the last crying blocks, enough glowstone, home before dark.\n\nMoss follows him to the conventional portal and stands in the threshold. The protagonist feeds him, leads him back to the green rug and gives the sit command. Moss complies after a second gesture and gives one short, impatient bark.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Nether days had their own routine. Moss followed him to the gate, accepted the food, and sat down only after being asked twice.\n\nThe protagonist leaves through the conventional obsidian portal.\n\nIts sound fades.\n\nHold a wide view of the shared room. Moss remains on the rug. The hearth crackles as it has throughout the montage.\n\nFire catches at the edge of the nearest green square. It moves out of the wide frame towards the timber floor.\n\nCut before the room is engulfed.\n\n**Production note.** The bark recorded here is reused subjectively in Scene 12. Do not imply that the fire path or sitting behaviour has been verified.\n\n---\n\n### 10. EXT./INT. R4 HOMESTEAD AND SEARCH ROUTES — NIGHT TO DAWN\n\n*Editorial target: 2:25*\n\n**Visual intent.** Resume Scene 1 exactly. Reveal an empty shared place rather than a graphic answer, then let the failed search narrow the protagonist's choices.\n\n**Audio intent.** Continue Scene 1's wind, footsteps and fire. After the wall breaks, suppress music and leave space for possible paws or a bark that never comes.\n\n**Action**\n\nResume the **MATCH PATH** run from Scene 1. The protagonist follows the path to the burning house.\n\nA storage window breaks outward. He passes recoverable chests without looking at them.\n\nAt the shared-room doorway, flame has reached the green wool beside the stone fireplace. Moss is not visible.\n\nThe protagonist empties his water bucket through the doorway. Steam fills the opening, but burning timber falls across the direct route.\n\nHe turns, breaks through the shortest plank wall and enters through the smoke.\n\nThe room is empty. The stone fireplace remains. The green rug is gone. A charred fan runs from its former position into the timber floor and wall.\n\nShow no body, collar drop or message.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The stone kept its shape. The place beside it did not.\n\nThe protagonist searches immediately. He checks behind surviving furniture, the crops, ridge, ravine path, conventional-portal shelter and the covered hollow beneath the window.\n\nHe carries Moss's food and repeatedly stops to listen.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He searched wherever Moss had ever waited: the crops, the ridge, the ravine path, the hollow beneath the window when thunder came.\n\nThe search continues until the fire subsides and birds begin.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Morning found him still listening.\n\nAt dawn, the protagonist returns to the ruins. The crying obsidian and glowstone still lie where he dropped them. From a scorched storage drawer, he retrieves the weathered Atlas.\n\nHe opens **FIRST THRESHOLD**. Beside the diagram is a small figure of a wolf accompanying the builder's clean journal.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The Atlas promised no rescue. It named one place he remembered for certain: the first threshold, with Moss beside him.\n\nHe turns to his unfinished safety calculations: another test, another measurement and an incomplete baffle.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The careful plan needed another test, another measure, another day. He closed that page.\n\nHe takes both journals, the crying obsidian and the glowstone, then descends towards the ravine.\n\n**Production note.** The visual burn fan is the causal evidence; narration does not announce the rug as dangerous before the fire. Moss's absence remains intentionally unresolved.\n\n---\n\n### 11. INT. R4 RAVINE — DAY TO NIGHT\n\n*Editorial target: 1:40*\n\n**Visual intent.** Answer the construction mystery through physical labour. Establish the incomplete protection and briefly plant the creeper's route without directing audience attention towards it.\n\n**Audio intent.** Build rhythm from descent, block placement and stone work. Let the echo texture return only as a faint musical memory until activation.\n\n**Action**\n\nDuring the descent, use one brief first-person shot. For less than a second, a muted green edge is visible at the extreme side of frame on a dark ledge. It withdraws behind the ravine wall before the protagonist centres his view. Add no sting or reaction.\n\nAt mining depth, the forked granite seam leads to the inert mouth.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The forked seam found the same place in every world. Here, it led him back to the mouth that had failed.\n\nUsing the seam, surface marker and recorded coordinates, the protagonist fills every missing position and completes a crying-obsidian frame. Its dimensions and small asymmetries match the R0 mouth.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Block by block, the impossible thing acquired an ordinary labour.\n\nHe places a respawn anchor according to the Atlas diagram and charges it with glowstone. The frame remains dark.\n\n**ATLAS INSERT**\n\nA keyed arrow runs from the completed source frame to **FIRST THRESHOLD**. At the receiving mouth, an unkeyed arrow continues to **NEXT MOUTH**.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Keyed by its builder, the frame should open on the first threshold. Anyone entering there without that key would still be carried forwards.\n\nThe protagonist builds a thick stone baffle offset from the frame. He tests whether he can reach the Atlas's ignition position while keeping most of his body behind cover.\n\nHis thumb returns to the torn measurement.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The torn page ended where safety should have begun.\n\nHe adds the thickest blocks that still leave the charged frame within reach.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The frame needed its builder close enough to wake it. He built all the distance his reach allowed.\n\nTorches cover the floor and working route. High behind the ignition position, the recessed ledge remains dark and partly hidden by the ravine wall.\n\nNight settles above. The protagonist puts both journals behind the baffle, equips flint and steel and faces the frame.\n\n**Production note.** The edge glimpse must be discoverable on rewatch without functioning as a reveal. Validate safe capture blocking separately. The creeper blast waking the charged echo frame remains invented canon.\n\n---\n\n### 12. INT. R4 RAVINE — NIGHT\n\n*Editorial target: 1:00*\n\n**Visual intent.** Reduce the final decision to one image and one remembered sound. Let an ordinary Minecraft hazard interrupt the planned ignition, then continue after the protagonist's death without narration.\n\n**Audio intent.** Remove the score. Use the muffled remembered bark, the creeper landing, its close hiss, the explosion and the delayed return of the portal texture.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist checks the **FIRST THRESHOLD** drawing, the charged anchor and the baffle.\n\nThe ravine room tone narrows. A single muffled bark returns in the mix: the same performance heard at the conventional gate in Scene 9, filtered as subjective memory rather than placed anywhere in the cave.\n\nThe protagonist pauses. He rests one hand on the wolf figure in the Atlas, closes the book and steps into reach of the frame.\n\nHe raises the flint and steel.\n\nA heavy landing sounds directly behind him. A creeper begins its close ignition hiss.\n\nThere is barely time for his shoulders to tighten.\n\nCut to black.\n\nThe creeper explodes.\n\nNo narration follows his death.\n\nAfter several seconds, return to a locked exterior plate. Smoke and debris fill the ravine. The crying frame still stands. The protagonist is absent; equipped items are scattered outside the baffle.\n\nThe charged anchor gives one brief violet flash inside the smoke.\n\nOne tear-like particle crosses the frame. Then another. Purple light gathers along the crying blocks, and the empty centre slowly resolves into a seamless view of the corresponding R0 cave—too late for its builder.\n\nThe original low portal texture returns.\n\n**Production note.** The fall, fatal blast, item scatter, frame survival, anchor flash and delayed destination view may require separate controlled plates. No vanilla behaviour is claimed for the echo activation.\n\n---\n\n### 13. THROUGH THE FRAME / R0 CLEARING — MORNING\n\n*Editorial target: 0:55*\n\n**Visual intent.** Reveal the source frame's destination, then repeat the ordinary mining departure whose meaning has changed. The protagonist does not survive or cross after death.\n\n**Audio intent.** Carry the portal texture through the frame, then restore the exact Scene 2 ambience and narration recording. Do not add a surface hum that attracts the earlier protagonist.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe camera remains after the protagonist's death. It approaches the active R4 frame as omniscient audience access, not his viewpoint or consciousness.\n\nThe R0 cave is already visible through the opening. The camera crosses, passes the matching forked granite seam and rises through stylised layers of stone and soil.\n\nIt reaches the surface in **MATCH FRAME A**.\n\nMorning. The earlier protagonist tightens his mining pack. His journal is still comparatively clean. Moss, now tamed and named, trots into the exact position beside him.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O. — IDENTICAL RECORDING FROM SCENE 2)**\n\n> Below the hill was only another day's work. They went together.\n\nThe protagonist starts down. Moss follows.\n\nCut underground to the complete crying-obsidian mouth already waiting beyond the granite seam. Rain falls through the seamless view into R1.\n\nCut to black.\n\n**Production note.** The through-ground move may use a purpose-built vertical set and matched dissolves. It must not imply post-death consciousness or a literal continuous camera path.\n\n**End.**\n","files":[{"path":".studio/internal/script/revision-pass-status.json","content":"{\n  \"schema_version\": 2,\n  \"draft_id\": \"draft-03\",\n  \"passes\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"promise\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Scenes 1–3 now move from the burning-home question to a clearing chosen from affection, the discovery and naming of Moss, and an impossible portal found through ordinary mining. Scenes 4–13 develop the echo mystery and connect the home, fire, source-frame construction and first-threshold destination.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Retain the quieter opening and cumulative visual evidence. Do not restore narration that announces isolation, danger or portal causality before the images earn it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"causality\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The need for iron causes the ravine descent; curiosity causes entry; the apparent dry return causes the second crossing; absent marks cause the echo deduction; the failing mouth causes settlement; material gathering causes the final absence; the established rug geometry supplies the proposed fire path; grief accelerates ignition; and the falling creeper's blast kills the protagonist while waking the charged source frame.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Retain each link and require production validation of wolf, fire, creeper, anchor and surviving-frame behaviour before capture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"dialogue_and_subtext\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The textual revision removes repeated plot hints and gives narration contrast, attitude or emotional compression rather than routine image description. The protagonist remains silent, Atlas labels remain concise and no narration knowingly confirms Moss's fate.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Use the revised restrained voice as the spoken-pass draft. Breath, cadence and repetition remain untested.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"visual_storytelling\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The solo arrival, taming and name-tag progression, repeated granite seam, absent marks, paired journals, directional Atlas arrows, growing rug, dropped cargo, creeper edge glimpse, frame geometry and repeated mining composition carry the principal deductions visually.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Keep the granite seam because it functions as a geographic coordinate. Use exact matched framing and do not replace physical evidence with explanatory narration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"production_feasibility\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The script-level review retains one avatar, one wolf and one narrator; groups work around a reusable ravine and house; avoids a second speaking role; and permits matched plates for crossings, fire, explosion and the final vertical transition.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Proceed only to storyboard and mechanics testing. Immersive Portals behaviour, fishing capture, wolf transfer, fire spread, creeper blocking and frame survival remain unverified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"continuity_and_canon\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Draft-03 preserves the R0–R4 echo chain, Weeping Atlas, R4 home and rug fire, fatal source-frame activation and R0 destination. The supplied comment changes R0 arrival so the protagonist meets Moss after choosing the clearing; Scene 13 consequently repeats the later pre-mining threshold composition.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Treat the revised Moss introduction and creeper-triggered activation as unlocked script proposals, not silent canon locks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"character_agency\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The protagonist chooses the clearing because he likes it, approaches and tames the wolf, enters the unexplained frame, attempts a return, constructs controlled tests, trusts only Atlas rules matching evidence, builds a home, leaves Moss according to an established routine and attempts the dangerous final ignition.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Retain the contrast between his evidence-led exploration and the decision to close the unfinished safety page after Moss disappears.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"escalation\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Each movement changes the problem from discovery to displacement, failed return, distinct echoes, incomplete knowledge, entrapment, chosen domestic stability, loss and a technically successful route that arrives after the builder's death.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Keep crossings limited to those that change the audience's model.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"theme_and_change\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The protagonist first chooses a place without needing it to solve a problem, then gradually builds a life around an escape project. After Moss disappears, the careful plan yields to the need to reach the one remembered moment where Moss was certainly present.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Let repeated domestic actions and the closed safety page carry the change; add no spoken moral or implication of restored life.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"information\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The seam establishes spatial identity; two missing marks establish separate histories; Atlas pages clarify tested one-way behaviour; keyed and unkeyed arrows distinguish the final routes; ordinary rug imagery precedes the visible burn fan; and frame construction plus the final ascent resolve the title question.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Keep the muffled bark explicitly subjective in production notes so it motivates memory without becoming false evidence of Moss nearby.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"scene_necessity\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Every scene supplies a promise, relationship step, causal test, state change, necessary instruction, home-building consequence, loss, final construction or destination pay-off. The revised Scene 2 montage now performs both settlement and Moss-introduction work.\",\n      \"decision\": \"If timing runs long after a real spoken pass, first compress montage coverage and repeated travel rather than removing the failed return, marker test, domestic Moss material or final match.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"audience_results_plan\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The QC record defines post-release questions for the opening transition, Moss-introduction clarity, echo-sequence retention, Atlas comprehension, fire-cause interpretation, ending-route understanding and packaging performance without assigning causation from graphs alone.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Use audience evidence to form hypotheses, then inspect the corresponding edit and comments before proposing a revision.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"},{"path":".studio/internal/script/table-read-notes.md","content":"# Table-read / spoken-pass notes — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n## Method and participants\n\nNo solo spoken read, multi-voice table read, storyboard read or animatic review has been conducted or supplied for `draft-03`.\n\nParticipants: none recorded.\n\nPlanned evidence: a solo spoken read for breath, cadence and provisional duration, followed by a storyboard or animatic review focused on echo geography, the subjective bark and the final route logic.\n\n## Clarity failures\n\nNo observed spoken-read failures can be recorded yet.\n\nItems to test:\n\n- whether the opening clearly communicates that the protagonist stays because he likes the clearing rather than because of the ravine or journal;\n- whether finding, taming and naming Moss reads as a progression rather than a continuity jump;\n- whether the forked granite seam functions as a repeated geographic coordinate without being mistaken for a supernatural symbol;\n- whether R1, R2 and R3 remain distinct without crew labels;\n- whether the two missing-marker shots establish stable, separate echoes;\n- whether the conventional Nether portal is plainly different from the crying frame;\n- whether keyed R4-to-R0 travel and unkeyed R0-to-R1 travel are understandable from the Atlas inserts;\n- whether the muffled bark in Scene 12 reads as memory rather than proof that Moss is physically nearby;\n- whether Moss's R4 fate reads as intentionally unknown rather than accidentally omitted.\n\n## Rhythm, breath, and repetitive dialogue\n\nNo performance evidence is available.\n\nTest the long opening sentence about choosing the clearing, the paired short lines in Scene 5, the Atlas explanation in Scene 6 and the keyed-route sentence in Scene 11. Check whether the domestic montage has enough silence between its five narration thoughts.\n\nThe repeated line in Scenes 2 and 13 must use one recording. Test whether its first use feels ordinary and its return feels transformed without a change in delivery.\n\n## Emotional transitions\n\nNo performed transition has been assessed.\n\nTest the movement from taming to naming Moss, temporary shelter to established home, routine Nether departure to fire, and failed search to the closed safety page. The final subjective bark should be restrained and brief; it must not become a sentimental command from beyond the frame.\n\n## Geography and visual comprehension\n\nNo storyboard or animatic evidence is available.\n\nThe review should track portal orientation, the forked granite seam, left-foot marks, R4 surface marker and the vertical relationship between the cave and clearing. Confirm that **MATCH FRAME A** is recognisably the later pre-mining moment from Scene 2, not the protagonist's first arrival at the clearing.\n\n## Exposition and subtext\n\nNo spoken exposition pass has occurred.\n\nThe revision removes narration about world isolation, an unexplained hum drawing the protagonist underground, the rug's dangerous distance and the house appearing safer. Check that the remaining narration adds interpretation or feeling without restating self-evident action.\n\nThe Atlas inserts remain the densest information. Test whether each label is readable before deciding whether any spoken explanation is necessary.\n\n## Production difficulty exposed by the read\n\nA read has not exposed production difficulty.\n\nKnown script-level risks to examine during storyboard and capture planning are the wolf taming montage, name-tag fishing beat, wolf portal blocking, five readable ravine states, matched cold-open continuation, reproducible fire geometry, the near-hidden creeper glimpse, surviving frame after the blast and final through-ground transition.\n\n## Changes made and unresolved decisions\n\nNo changes are attributed to a table read or spoken pass.\n\nDraft-03 implements the supplied comments by making the protagonist choose the clearing because he likes it; introducing, taming and naming Moss during the montage; replacing the portal-hum summons with an ordinary mining need; reducing explanatory hints; clarifying the granite seam's visual purpose; removing narration that flags the rug and house as dangerous; and replacing the climax memory montage with a muffled remembered bark.\n\nUnresolved decisions include the target Minecraft edition and version, mod compatibility, validated fire and blast geometry, Moss's intentionally unseen fate, final death presentation, whether the subjective bark reads correctly and any duration trims identified by an actual read."},{"path":".studio/internal/script/pronunciation-guide.md","content":"# Pronunciation and language guide\n\n- Delivery accent: creator's natural English; do not imitate an accent.\n- Spelling convention: British English.\n- These cues support consistency only; the creator's natural, intelligible delivery takes priority.\n\n## Names and terms\n\n- **Moss** — /mɒs/; short vowel, like the plant.\n- **ravine** — /rə-VEE-n/; stress the second syllable.\n- **obsidian** — /əb-SID-ee-ən/.\n- **crying obsidian** — /KRY-ing əb-SID-ee-ən/; keep the two words separate.\n- **Nether** — /NETH-ə/ in a non-rhotic delivery; use the creator's natural final sound.\n- **respawn** — /ree-SPAWN/.\n- **glowstone** — /GLOH-stohn/.\n- **echo** — /EK-oh/; plural **echoes**, /EK-ohz/.\n- **Atlas** — /AT-ləs/. The production name **Weeping Atlas** is /WEE-ping AT-ləs/.\n- **threshold** — /THRESH-hohld/.\n- **mouths** — /maʊðz/ in **one frame, many mouths**; use the voiced final sound.\n- **baffle** — /BAF-əl/.\n- **ignition** — /ig-NISH-ən/.\n\n## Delivery and emphasis\n\n- **He liked the place. So he put his pack down.** — keep plain and decisive; do not romanticise the pause.\n- **Once could be loss. Twice was a rule.** — allow the second sentence to land without raising volume.\n- **Same numbers. Wrong history.** — use a short pause; do not overplay **wrong**.\n- **Next mouth. Never previous.** — clear informational delivery, with light stress on **next** and **never**.\n- **The repair was his. So was the hand. The years between them were not.** — keep the three clauses measured rather than ominous.\n- **The torn page ended where safety should have begun.** — stress **safety** lightly; avoid announcing the later death.\n- **Below the hill was only another day's work. They went together.** — use the identical recording in Scenes 2 and 13. The first delivery should remain ordinary enough to acquire its second meaning through the image.\n- There is no narration after the protagonist's death.\n\n## Crew-only labels\n\n- `R0` to `R4` are pronounced **ar-zero** to **ar-four** in production discussion.\n- They do not appear in narration or on-screen story text.\n\n## Translation notes\n\nNo translated dialogue is currently planned. Preserve the distinction between a conventional Nether portal, an unkeyed echo mouth and the builder-keyed final frame in any subtitles or localisation.\n\nThe Scene 12 bark is a subjective repetition of the Scene 9 sound, not dialogue, a supernatural message or proof of Moss's location."},{"path":".studio/internal/script/script-qc.md","content":"# Script QC — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n> Complete the twelve specialised passes separately. Record machine-readable status in `revision-pass-status.json`; use this document for evidence, judgement, and required changes.\n\nScope of this record: textual review of `draft-03` only. It is not evidence of a table read, animatic, capture test, Minecraft mechanics test or audience response.\n\n## 1. Promise\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe cold open promises a developed home in danger and unexplained crying-obsidian cargo. The opening proper replaces that emergency with a clearing the protagonist chooses because he likes it, then lets him find, tame and name Moss through ordinary survival activity. Mining for iron produces the crying portal without a summons or narrated warning.\n\nThe main story fulfils the echo-reality promise through recognisable geography, failed return, practical marker tests, incomplete personal knowledge and a final source frame that creates the mouth beneath the earlier clearing. The script avoids an ordinary Nether adventure, hidden authority, second speaking character and explanatory final speech.\n\n## 2. Causality\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe principal links are:\n\n- liking the clearing causes the protagonist to settle before it offers a mystery;\n- early survival supplies the bones, iron, fishing routine and eventual bond with Moss;\n- the need for iron causes the ordinary ravine descent;\n- curiosity about the visible destination causes entry;\n- dry stone in the returning view makes the mistaken return reasonable;\n- the failed return causes a controlled marker test;\n- two missing marks establish separate histories and make the Atlas's tested rules credible;\n- the blocked stair and failing mouth cause the last uncontrolled crossing;\n- incomplete materials cause shelter, settlement and gradual gathering;\n- the established rug growth supplies the proposed fire path without verbal warning;\n- the last material need causes the Nether absence;\n- the established sit routine keeps Moss in the shared room;\n- the empty room and failed search cause the protagonist to close the unfinished safety plan;\n- the Atlas's proximity rule places him beside the charged frame;\n- the unlit ledge supplies the falling creeper;\n- its blast kills him and, under invented echo canon, wakes the source frame;\n- the keyed destination creates the R0 mouth shown earlier.\n\nFire spread, wolf behaviour, creeper timing, anchor response and frame survival remain proposed links until tested in the selected version.\n\n## 3. Character agency\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe protagonist chooses the clearing for pleasure rather than plot utility. He approaches and tames the wolf, brings Moss into his routines, enters the portal, attempts a return, constructs stronger tests and trusts the Atlas only where it matches evidence.\n\nIn R4 he chooses shelter, lets temporary walls become a home, gathers return materials cautiously and leaves Moss according to an established Nether routine. After the fire, he searches through the night before choosing the dangerous first-threshold attempt. The creeper controls the exact fatal interruption, but the protagonist's decision to complete, key and approach the frame creates the climactic situation and delayed route.\n\n## 4. Escalation\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nEscalation changes the problem rather than merely increasing spectacle:\n\n1. an unexplained object interrupts ordinary mining;\n2. a changed ravine creates displacement;\n3. the failed return removes reversibility;\n4. absent marks establish distinct echoes;\n5. the Atlas offers incomplete and dangerous knowledge;\n6. the collapsing mouth creates entrapment;\n7. a chosen home creates something meaningful to lose;\n8. Moss's absence turns careful research into urgency;\n9. an ordinary hostile mob interrupts the final technical plan;\n10. successful activation arrives after its builder can use it.\n\nThe number of crossings remains limited to those that change the audience's model.\n\n## 5. Theme and character change\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe protagonist begins by choosing a place simply because he wants to stay. In R4 he repeats that capacity under constraint: an intended camp becomes a home while escape is gathered in pieces. This makes the later loss more than failure of a mechanism.\n\nHis practical patience is visible in marker tests, measured collecting and baffle sketches. Grief does not erase that history; it causes him to close the page requiring more time. The route succeeds technically but cannot return the lived relationship he wanted. No narration states a moral or implies that death restores Moss.\n\n## 6. Information design\n\nTextual review complete.\n\n| Audience question | Fair evidence before resolution | Resolution or intended residue |\n|---|---|---|\n| Why does he stay at the clearing? | He pauses, enjoys the place and puts down his pack before using the ravine | Narration confirms that he simply likes it |\n| Where did Moss come from? | Wild-wolf sighting, bones, taming hearts, shared routines, fishing and anvil | Scene 2 shows finding, taming and naming as separate steps |\n| Who made the R0 portal? | No construction route, matched source-frame asymmetry, Atlas diagram and R4 materials | The protagonist builds the R4 source that projects the R0 mouth |\n| Are the caves the same place? | Identical forked seam, coordinates and ravine profile | Repetition establishes shared geography across echoes |\n| Are the echoes one changing world? | Incompatible ecology and two absent adjacent-block marks | Marker tests establish separate histories |\n| Why does returning fail? | Directional particles and two forward crossings | Atlas confirms unkeyed travel goes to the next mouth |\n| Why can the final frame reach R0? | Builder-key and first-threshold arrows | The builder-keyed source selects R0 |\n| Why does the R0 entrant still reach R1? | Separate unkeyed arrow at the receiving mouth | The earlier protagonist enters without the builder's key |\n| What caused the fire? | Open hearth, Moss's repeated rug choice and its gradual extension | The later burn fan begins at the missing rug position |\n| Did Moss die in R4? | Empty room, failed search and no response | Intentionally unresolved; no false confirmation is supplied |\n| Is the Scene 12 bark real? | It repeats the Scene 9 performance and is mixed subjectively | Production direction defines it as memory, not location evidence |\n| Did the protagonist survive? | Fatal blast and scattered equipment | He dies; the later camera is omniscient presentation |\n| Who completed the older Atlas? | Matching repair, handwriting and clean counterpart | Intentionally open reflective residue |\n\nThe narrator states beliefs or emotional compression available at each stage and no longer announces clues before the image.\n\n## 7. Scene necessity\n\nTextual review complete.\n\n- Scene 1 supplies the home-in-danger question paid off in Scene 10.\n- Scene 2 establishes chosen place, Moss's origin, shared routines and the final repeated composition.\n- Scene 3 makes ordinary mining collide with the impossible portal.\n- Scene 4 makes the failed return reasonable.\n- Scene 5 converts confusion into tested evidence.\n- Scene 6 supplies incomplete knowledge tied to the protagonist's own methods.\n- Scene 7 changes travel into settlement.\n- Scene 8 earns home, relationship, return materials and the unannounced fire path.\n- Scene 9 establishes the separation and ignition.\n- Scene 10 converts absence into the final objective.\n- Scene 11 constructs the answer, establishes proximity and plants the creeper route.\n- Scene 12 resolves the attempt, death and delayed activation.\n- Scene 13 pays off the destination and repeated mining departure.\n\nIf a real timed read runs long, first compress work montage, travel coverage and pauses. Removing the failed return, marker tests, domestic Moss material, fire continuation or final destination would break causality or pay-off.\n\n## 8. Dialogue, exposition, and subtext\n\nTextual review complete; spoken evidence pending.\n\nThere is one narrator and no essential borrowed voice. The revision removes lines that explicitly announce world isolation, use a hum to summon the protagonist, flag the wool as dangerous or describe the house as safer. Narration now adds character attitude, mistaken interpretation, compressed time or emotional contrast.\n\nAtlas exposition remains confined to short labels and interpretations supported by prior marker evidence. The repeated Scene 2 line acquires a second meaning in Scene 13 without changed wording. The subjective bark replaces a narrated memory montage and carries no propositional information.\n\nA spoken pass must still test the opening cadence, Scene 5's clipped deductions, the Atlas explanation, the keyed-route sentence and spacing between domestic montage lines.\n\n## 9. Visual storytelling\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nPrimary visual carriers are:\n\n- the match cut from R4 emergency to R0 arrival;\n- the solo arrival and deliberate choice to put down the pack;\n- taming hearts, collar, name tag and anvil for Moss's progression;\n- the forked granite seam for geographic identity;\n- water, vegetation and ash for divergent histories;\n- two missing constructed marks for the echo deduction;\n- matching journal repairs and handwriting for the Atlas connection;\n- directional arrows and particles for one-way travel;\n- ordinary and crying portal designs for system distinction;\n- Moss's repeated rug choice and gradual rug growth for affection and later causality;\n- dropped cargo for cold-open continuity;\n- the near-hidden ledge glimpse for the creeper's route;\n- matched crying-frame geometry for the builder reveal;\n- the exact pre-mining composition for the final reinterpretation.\n\nAn animatic must verify that these remain legible without crew labels or explanatory captions.\n\n## 10. Production feasibility\n\nScript-level review complete; execution evidence pending.\n\nThe draft uses one avatar, one wolf and one narrator. It avoids visible counterparts and supporting dialogue. Principal sets are one ravine in five states, one compact house in lived, burning and ruined states, one conventional portal and several crying-frame states.\n\nFeasibility controls include locked marker close-ups, repeated seam framing, a limited wolf montage, an incomplete local mouth, readable Atlas inserts, separate blast plates and a purpose-built vertical transition rather than a literal continuous underground move.\n\nTesting is still required for:\n\n- the taming and name-tag capture sequence;\n- tamed-wolf entry and arrival blocking;\n- sitting behaviour during the Nether absence;\n- exact wool-to-timber fire geometry;\n- Immersive Portals compatibility and parallax capture;\n- the dark-ledge creeper route and safe performance blocking;\n- charged-frame response and survival geometry;\n- safe explosion compositing;\n- readable resets across five echo states.\n\n## 11. Continuity and canon\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe current unlocked Story plan has the protagonist and Moss arrive together. The supplied script comment explicitly requests that the wolf instead be found, tamed and named during the montage. Draft-03 implements that request as an unlocked script proposal. Consequently, Scene 13 repeats the later pre-mining threshold composition rather than the first arrival shot.\n\nThe echo chain, Weeping Atlas, R4 domestic fire, Moss's unresolved absence, fatal source-frame activation and R0 destination remain intact. The falling-creeper interruption already present in the current script is retained and visually planted as requested, but its activation rule remains speculative and unlocked.\n\nActive continuity items are **MATCH PATH**, **MATCH FRAME A**, the granite seam, portal orientation, cobblestone counts, both journals, name-tag timing, green rug growth, conventional-portal distinction, dropped cargo, broken wall, Atlas tear, baffle geometry, dark ledge and source-frame asymmetry.\n\nNo canon is treated as locked.\n\n## 12. Audience-results plan\n\nPlan complete; no audience evidence exists.\n\nAfter release, investigate rather than assume:\n\n- **Opening retention:** Do viewers remain through the fire-to-clearing match cut and the quieter settlement montage?\n- **Moss introduction:** Do comments indicate that viewers understood the wolf was found, tamed and named over time?\n- **Echo sequence:** Is there a decline during R1–R3, and does edit inspection suggest repetition, geography confusion or another cause?\n- **Atlas comprehension:** Do viewers distinguish next-mouth travel from the builder-keyed first threshold without supplemental explanation?\n- **Fire interpretation:** Do comments identify the rug and hearth path, or do viewers treat the fire as arbitrary?\n- **Subjective bark:** Do viewers read the sound as memory, nearby survival evidence or supernatural communication?\n- **Ending comprehension:** Do viewers understand that the protagonist built the connected source frame, died, and did not personally cross afterwards?\n- **Emotional result:** Do responses discuss Moss and the made home rather than only the portal mechanism?\n- **Packaging:** Compare title and thumbnail performance with introduction retention before attributing any audience loss to the script.\n\nA graph, spike or dip does not establish cause. Form a hypothesis, inspect the corresponding edit and comments, then record any proposed revision separately."}],"questions":[],"warnings":["No table read, solo spoken pass, storyboard review or animatic review is evidenced; all performance and comprehension notes remain pending.","Immersive Portals compatibility, wolf transfer and sitting behaviour, name-tag capture, fire spread, creeper blocking, charged-frame response and surviving-frame geometry remain unverified.","Finding Moss after the protagonist arrives shifts the final repeated image from the original arrival decision to the later pre-mining threshold; this is documented as an unlocked script-level change, not a canon lock.","The Scene 12 bark is written as subjective memory. If sound design makes it appear diegetic, it could falsely imply that Moss is alive nearby and undermine the final decision."]}
